{
  "image_name": "632b08a642e85fb2cb8fbc6b19ab29d8.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares sulfur and selenium chemistry in proteins, emphasizing how replacing S with Se changes redox behavior. On the left, a schematic shows X–H groups attached to a peptide or protein, where X can be sulfur or selenium. A table highlights key physical and chemical differences: selenium has a lower pKa, a more negative redox potential, larger atomic size, longer C–X and X–H bonds, and a weaker X–H bond than sulfur. Overall, these values suggest selenium is more easily ionized and more reactive in redox processes.\n\nThe right side summarizes the oxidation cycle of sulfur- and selenium-containing residues. The reduced form XH can interconvert with a dimeric disulfide/diselenide form, and can be oxidized stepwise to XOH, XO2−, and XO3−. The labels indicate contrasting kinetics: selenium appears to oxidize faster in the early steps to selenenic and seleninic states, while sulfur oxidizes faster in later overoxidation steps toward sulfinic and sulfonic forms. Reduction by thiols is also marked as slow for sulfur but fast for selenium. Taken together, the figure conveys that selenium-containing protein groups are chemically distinct from sulfur analogs and can cycle through redox states more readily and reversibly.",
